  "text": "As the problem is presented, I will reason through the steps required to solve it:\n\n1. **Understanding the problem**: The problem is asking for the smallest integer that is in the range of the function \\(g(x)\\) but not in the range of \\(g(-x)\\).\n\n2. **Defining the function \\(g(x)\\)**: The function \\(g(x)\\) is defined in two parts:\n    - For \\(x < 1\\), \\(g(x) = \\left\\lfloor x^2 - 2x \\right\\rfloor\\)\n    - For \\(x > 1\\), \\(g(x) = \\left\\lceil x^2 - 2x \\right\\rceil\\)\n\n3. **Analyzing the range of \\(g(x)\\)**:\n    - For \\(x < 1\\), \\(x^2 - 2x\\) is a quadratic function that opens upwards and has a vertex at \\(x = 1\\). As \\(x\\) approaches 1 from the left, \\(x^2 - 2x\\) approaches 1 (since \\(1^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 = -1\\)), and as \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\), \\(x^2 - 2x\\) also approaches \\(\\infty\\). Therefore, the range of \\(g(x)\\) for \\(x < 1\\) is all integers greater than or equal to 1.\n    - For \\(x > 1\\), \\(x^2 - 2x\\) is also a quadratic function that opens upwards and has a vertex at \\(x = 1\\). As \\(x\\) approaches 1 from the right, \\(x^2 - 2x\\) approaches 1 (since \\(1^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 = -1\\)), and as \\(x\\) approaches \\(\\infty\\), \\(x^2 - 2x\\) approaches \\(\\infty\\). Therefore, the range of \\(g(x)\\) for \\(x > 1\\) is all integers greater than or equal to 1.\n\n4. **Analyzing the range of \\(g(-x)\\)**:\n    - For \\(x < 1\\), \\(-x > 1\\), so \\(g(-x) = \\left\\lceil (-x)^2 - 2(-x) \\right\\rceil = \\left\\lceil x^2 + 2x \\right\\rceil\\). As \\(x\\) approaches 1 from the left, \\(x^2 + 2x\\) approaches 3 (since \\(1^2 + 2 \\cdot 1 = 3\\)), and as \\(x\\) approaches \\(-\\infty\\), \\(x^2 + 2x\\) also approaches \\(\\infty\\). Therefore, the range of \\(g(-x)\\) for \\(x < 1\\) is all integers greater than or equal to 3.\n    - For \\(x > 1\\), \\(-x < 1\\), so \\(g(-x) = \\left\\lfloor (-x)^2 - 2(-x) \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor x^2 + 2x \\right\\rfloor\\). As \\(x\\) approaches 1 from the right, \\(x^2 + 2x\\) approaches 3 (since \\(1^2 + 2 \\cdot 1 = 3\\)), and as \\(x\\) approaches \\(\\infty\\), \\(x^2 + 2x\\) also approaches \\(\\infty\\). Therefore, the range of \\(g(-x)\\) for \\(x > 1\\) is all integers greater than or equal to 3.\n\n5. **Finding the smallest integer in the range of \\(g(x)\\) but not in the range of \\(g(-x)\\)**: The range of \\(g(x)\\) includes all integers greater than or equal to 1, and the range of \\(g(-x)\\) includes all integers greater than or equal to 3. The smallest integer that is in the range of \\(g(x)\\) but not in the range of \\(g(-x)\\) is 2.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
